-- title:
--   Lemma 3.2 — for a normalized instance the shared-participant order is the full rotation order
-- statement:
--   If $I$ is normalized, then the rotation poset $Q$ of a maximal chain and the full rotation poset $Q_f$ coincide: for distinct rotations, $\rho\preceq\sigma$ if and only if $\rho\prec_f\sigma$.
--
--   The Remark on p.10 of the paper notes that the two orders can differ for an arbitrary instance. For normalized instances the whole order is generated by each person's sequence of rotations, which is what the potential argument uses.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: an instance has n ≥ 1 men and n women → every n : ℕ; reason: n = 0 has no people, one empty matching and only the empty member, and every clause holds trivially; for n ≥ 1 it is the same statement
--   - encoding: Q ∈ 𝒬: the rotation poset of a normalized instance with a chosen maximal chain, whose elements are rotations → Q : Member n, whose rotations are the chain steps Fin Q.R; reason: the convention of p.12; Proposition 2.3 (i) makes steps and rotations one-to-one, and F_h reads degrees, which a bare poset does not determine
--   - encoding: "Q = Q_f", as posets on Π → ∃ rot : Fin Q.R → Rot n naming the step rotations, a bijection onto Π, with Q.le i j ↔ FullLT (rot i) (rot j) for i ≠ j; reason: the order ≺f is defined on distinct rotations; rot is the identification of Proposition 2.3 (i)
-- source:
--   Ghorbani, Mnich, A 2.81^n upper bound on the number of stable matchings, arXiv:2611.06494 (held PDF sha256 9445f6a8…), Lemma 3.2, p.11 (PDF p.12)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_StableCount_Member

namespace StableCount

open StableMatching

/-- Lemma 3.2 (arXiv:2611.06494, held PDF sha256 9445f6a8…, Lemma 3.2, p.11). If `I` is normalized, then `Q = Q_f`: the shared-participant
order of the chain and the full rotation order coincide.

**Formalization Note** "`I` is normalized" is a field of `Member`. The rotations of `Q` are chain steps,
and `rot` identifies them with `Π`, as Proposition 2.3 (i) does. -/
theorem lemma_3_2 {n : ℕ} (Q : Member n) :
    ∃ rot : Fin Q.R → Rot n,
      (∀ i, IsExposed Q.I (Q.μ i.castSucc) (rot i) ∧
        IsElim (Q.μ i.castSucc) (rot i) (Q.μ i.succ)) ∧
      Function.Injective rot ∧ Set.range rot = Pi Q.I ∧
      ∀ i j, i ≠ j → (Q.le i j ↔ FullLT Q.I (rot i) (rot j)) := by sorry

end StableCount
